Technical Decisions

Group 1 · Santeri Karjalainen, Zohaib Salman

The docking station sends infrared signals that tell the robot whether it is to the left of, to the right of, or on the line leading straight into the dock. The robot reads these signals and steers itself in.

1. How the station sends its signals

Options:

Taking turns: the left and right beams send their signals one after the other.
Sending together: both beams send at the same moment. Where they overlap, the two signals combine into a third, "centre" signal.

Trade-off: Taking turns is simpler and easier to get working, but the robot gets updates more slowly. Sending together gives roughly twice as many updates, but the timing between the beams has to match exactly.

Decision: Try both. Start with taking turns, then move to sending together as the final method.

Reason: Taking turns is the safest way to first prove the signal works at all. Sending together should make docking faster and smoother, and testing both lets us compare them with real measurements.

2. One circuit for both methods

Options: Build a separate circuit for each method, or one circuit that can do both.

Trade-off: The taking-turns method could use a slightly simpler circuit. A circuit that supports sending together can also take turns.

Decision: Build one circuit, where each beam is controlled separately by the microcontroller. Switch between the methods by changing the program only.

Reason: No rewiring is needed. Both methods are tested on exactly the same hardware, so the comparison is fair.

3. Ready-made IR receivers

Options: Build our own receiver from light sensors and amplifiers, or use ready-made IR receiver modules like the ones in TVs.

Trade-off: A custom receiver could measure signal strength but is sensitive to noise and needs tuning. Ready-made modules give a clean on/off signal that is easy to read.

Decision: Ready-made receiver modules.

Reason: They filter out sunlight, lamps and other interference by themselves, which removes most of the risk.

4. One-way communication

Options: Let the station and robot talk to each other (for example over Bluetooth), or have the station only broadcast.

Decision: The station only broadcasts; the robot makes all the decisions.

Reason: Simpler and more reliable. No pairing or radio setup is needed, and the station does not need to know anything about the robot.

5. Divider wall between the beams

Options: Let the left and right beams overlap naturally, or put a wall between them.

Trade-off: Without a wall, the beams overlap over a wide and unpredictable area, so the robot thinks it is centred when it is not.

Decision: A 3D-printed divider wall between the left and right LEDs.

Reason: The wall creates a sharp, narrow centre line that stays the same width at different distances.

6. Two receivers on the robot

Options: One receiver or two.

Trade-off: The station's signal tells the robot which side of the centre line it is on, but not which way it is facing.

Decision: Two receivers, side by side.

Reason: Comparing what the two receivers see tells the robot whether it is pointing straight at the dock, so it can both find the centre line and line itself up before backing in.

7. A separate short-range signal

Decision: A third, weak beam that can only be seen close to the dock.

Reason: It tells the robot when it is close enough to lower its charging contacts.
